    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 204,
      "text": "MEXICO\n15. Inflation is projected to plateau at around 8½ Chain-Weighted Wage Growth\n(Y/Y)\npercent in the second half of 2022 and then decline\n16\ngradually. Core and headline inflation are expected to\n12\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 204,
      "end_char": 441,
      "text": "return to the midpoint of the target band in mid-2024. This\n8\nassumes that the policy rate will be raised to around 10\n4\npercent by early 2023 and be maintained at that level 0\nbefore being gradually reduced starting in late 2023. On -4\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 441,
      "end_char": 636,
      "text": "balance, risks to this inflation outlook are judged to be -8\ntilted to the upside.\nAll IMSS Formal (RHS)\n16. Economic activity is projected to pick up in 2024 Formal INEGI Overall (RHS)\nInformal\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 636,
      "end_char": 786,
      "text": "and the following years, in tandem with the U.S. The\nSources: ENOE and IMFstaff calculations.\noutput gap will narrow after widening somewhat in 2023.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 786,
      "end_char": 983,
      "text": "However, without significant structural reforms, the growth rebound is expected to be modest, to\nabout 2 percent. Potential output growth per capita would recover to a rate only slightly above one\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 983,
      "end_char": 1191,
      "text": "percent.5 The current account deficit is projected to stabilize at around 1 percent of GDP in the\nmedium term.\n17. The balance of risks to the near-term outlook for economic growth is tilted to the\ndownside.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1191,
      "end_char": 1384,
      "text": "• Higher global inflation and a sharper tightening of global financial conditions, or a sharper\ndeceleration in economic global or U.S. growth, are the main downside risks. Additional external\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1384,
      "end_char": 1575,
      "text": "prices shocks, including from the Russian invasion of Ukraine, could put upward pressure on\ninflation, especially for food items, and inflation expectations. An illustrative global inflation\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1575,
      "end_char": 1760,
      "text": "scenario presented in the July 2022 World Economic Outlook Update could result in a peak\noutput loss of 2 percent or more and stalling real GDP in Mexico (Box 2). Tighter-than-expected\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1760,
      "end_char": 1938,
      "text": "monetary policy could lead to an upswing in capital outflows, potential system-wide liquidity\nstress, downward pressure on the peso, and higher imported inflation. This could be\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 1938,
      "end_char": 2122,
      "text": "exacerbated by a fall in remittances as the U.S. economy slows or worsening competitiveness\nrelative to competitors that have seen a real depreciation in recent months. A sharper U.S.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2122,
      "end_char": 2362,
      "text": "slowdown would be particularly consequential—in the short-term Mexican GDP has an elasticity\nof more than one with that of the U.S. (Annex VII).\n• On the domestic side, the inflation surge might reverse more slowly than expected, as it may\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2362,
      "end_char": 2536,
      "text": "have led to more backward-looking price dynamics or impending further increases in inflation\nexpectations. In both cases, a tighter monetary policy stance would be required.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2536,
      "end_char": 2724,
      "text": "• Risks loom along other dimensions as well. The emergence of new COVID-19 variants with\ngreater evasion of previous immunity or higher hospitalization and mortality rates could result in\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00015",
      "start_char": 2724,
      "end_char": 2933,
      "text": "lower mobility and higher precautionary saving. Additional large negative supply shocks from\n5 Average annual population growth is projected at 0.7 percent per year in 2022-27. Potential GDP growth after 2024\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00016",
      "start_char": 2933,
      "end_char": 2996,
      "text": "would thus be about 1.8 percent.\n10 INTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
  "source_id": "MAC-010",
  "source_sha256": "8fac4146590dbe2ed458f7356748f5880d021e03cc4982b1e7efe083d33f622a",
  "locator": "page 15",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 197,
      "text": "MEXICO\nChina could also be in train if continued zero COVID-19 policies motivated lockdowns similar to\nthose seen this year. Rising inflation, declining incomes, and worsening crime and inequality\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 197,
      "end_char": 429,
      "text": "could lead to social unrest and political instability.\n• Among medium-term risks are the possibility that scarring from the pandemic or policy\nuncertainty lowers potential output, as could climate-change induced risks (such as more\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 429,
      "end_char": 662,
      "text": "frequent severe hurricanes and floods, or earlier emergence—or larger amounts of—stranded\nassets in the carbon-intensive energy sector).\n• Upside risks include the continuation of nearshoring dynamics in Mexico or smaller spillovers\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 662,
      "end_char": 840,
      "text": "from an economic slowdown in the U.S. than was seen in the past. The former entails the\nbuildup of productive and distribution capacity in Mexico, with a view to serve the North\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 840,
      "end_char": 1022,
      "text": "American markets. The latter could reflect continued robust service sector performance as the\ndomestic economy recovers, or delayed effects on the manufacturing sector from the U.S.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 1022,
      "end_char": 1215,
      "text": "slowdown. Mexico’s exports could also get some boost in the near-term due to trade diversion\neffects from geopolitical tensions (Annex VIII, Annex VII of 2021 Staff Report).\nAuthorities’ Views\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1215,
      "end_char": 1390,
      "text": "18. The authorities agreed that the current conjuncture of high and rising global and\ndomestic interest rates, high inflation, and downside risks to growth presented elevated\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1390,
      "end_char": 1587,
      "text": "uncertainty with risks tilted to the downside. The budget contemplates growth for 2023 in the\nrange of 1.2 to 3.0 percent in 2023, with a central estimate of 2.1, while the latest Banxico forecast\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1587,
      "end_char": 1785,
      "text": "puts it between 0.8 and 2.4 percent with a central estimate of 1.6 percent. The authorities see\ninflation falling in 2023 from a high level under the normalization of supply shocks, fiscal measures\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1785,
      "end_char": 1976,
      "text": "to contain fuel prices pass-through, and decisive monetary policy. They agreed disappointing\ngrowth in the U.S. is the headline risk. However, they noted that the manufacturing sector, which\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 1976,
      "end_char": 2168,
      "text": "represents the bulk of trade exposure, might this time be less affected than services in a U.S.\nslowdown. At the same time, they expected that Mexico could benefit from the nearshoring trend.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2168,
      "end_char": 2402,
      "text": "Faster-than-expected global monetary tightening could also spark a steepening credit curve and\npressures on capital flows, though experience through the tightening cycle so far attenuates these\nconcerns.\nINTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND 11"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
  "source_id": "MAC-010",
  "source_sha256": "8fac4146590dbe2ed458f7356748f5880d021e03cc4982b1e7efe083d33f622a",
  "locator": "page 16",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 159,
      "text": "MEXICO\nBox 2. Global Tightening and Inflation Scenario\nA global tightening and inflation scenario was simulated with the IMF’s G20MOD multi-country DSGE model\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 159,
      "end_char": 391,
      "text": "to better understand the consequences of multiple downside shocks on the Mexican economy.1 The model\nresults are presented in cumulative layers, with each successive layer adding another shock on top of those\nin the previous layer.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 391,
      "end_char": 611,
      "text": "An initial layer simulates the direct effect of a temporary shock to global oil and food prices, which would\nrise by 10 and 50 percent, respectively, relative to the baseline. The shock would raise headline inflation in\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 611,
      "end_char": 829,
      "text": "Mexico by nearly 1 percent above an already elevated baseline in year-on-year terms for a few quarters, but\nit would only have limited direct effects on output in the absence of a significant monetary policy response.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 829,
      "end_char": 1050,
      "text": "In a second layer, the commodity shock on top of the baseline, which already includes inflation at a 20-year\nhigh, causes a temporary dislocation of inflation expectations globally, including in Mexico. This results in a\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 1050,
      "end_char": 1267,
      "text": "sharper increase in the domestic (and global) policy interest rate, in line with the increase in inflation\nexpectations, to bring expectations back in line with the inflation target and limit second-round effects. In\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1267,
      "end_char": 1485,
      "text": "this case, output levels fall substantially below baseline, peaking at more than 1 percent below in the first\nhalf of 2023. Higher policy rates also present elevated systemic liquidity risk, discussed in detail in the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1485,
      "end_char": 1662,
      "text": "accompanying 2022 Financial System Stability Assessment (FSSA) for Mexico.\nLast, a third layer us added with a 1 percent decline in domestic demand, inspired by diminished real\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1662,
      "end_char": 1866,
      "text": "earnings expectations, or perhaps an un-modeled credit contraction. This brings output to more than\n2percent below baseline at the trough while somewhat attenuating prices pressures. The three layers put\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1866,
      "end_char": 2097,
      "text": "substantial cumulative pressure on the corporate interest rate though a capital flow shock is not modeled\ndirectly.\n1 The simulation is consistent with the global downside scenario presented in the July 2022 World Economic Outlook\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 2097,
      "end_char": 2313,
      "text": "Update. It was constructed on the basis of shocks to economic variables relative from their baseline levels and, as such,\nthe results generally are not sensitive to the baseline levels.\n12 INTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
  "source_id": "MAC-010",
  "source_sha256": "8fac4146590dbe2ed458f7356748f5880d021e03cc4982b1e7efe083d33f622a",
  "locator": "page 17",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 159,
      "text": "MEXICO\nPOLICY DISCUSSIONS\nA. Tackling High Inflation\nMonetary Policy\n19. Banxico has taken a proactive approach to address increasingly broad-based inflation.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 159,
      "end_char": 358,
      "text": "Since June 2021, successive and gradually larger increases in the reference rate brought the ex ante\nreal policy rate (i.e., the nominal rate adjusted for one-year ahead inflation expectations) to a\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 358,
      "end_char": 556,
      "text": "moderately restrictive level by September 2022 at about 4 percent.6 This has been an appropriately\ncalibrated response to the upward surprises to inflation and sequential delays in returning to the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 556,
      "end_char": 764,
      "text": "inflation target. In recent monetary policy statements, Banxico has also rightly indicated its intention\nto further increase the policy rate. It is expected that a policy rate of at least 10 percent would be\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 764,
      "end_char": 988,
      "text": "required for inflation to reverse close to the central bank’s 3-percent target within the usual two\nyears or so.\nInflation and Expectations\nReal Ex-Ante Policy Rates\n(Seasonally adjusted, y/y)\n9 (Percent)\n10\nBrazil\nHeadline\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 988,
      "end_char": 1181,
      "text": "8 Inflation 8 Chile\nMexico\n7 Core 6 Peru\nInflation\nColombia\n4\n6 Expectations\n: 12 Months 2\n5\nExpectations\n0\n: 1-4 Years\n4 -2\nExpectations\n: 5-8 Years\n3 -4\nSources: Banxico and Haver Analytics.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1181,
      "end_char": 1373,
      "text": "20. Inflation risks are expected to remain heightened for some time. The proactive policy\ntightening already put in place, alongside further increases in the policy rate, and weakening global\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1373,
      "end_char": 1573,
      "text": "demand are expected to lead to a slowdown in activity and a decline in inflation. However, there is\nsignificant uncertainty about the timing, speed, and durability of the downward path for inflation.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1573,
      "end_char": 1778,
      "text": "This uncertainty arises from the possibility of: (i) further shocks, including spillovers to local raw and\nprocessed food prices from global commodity prices and domestic food prices, especially resulting\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1778,
      "end_char": 1973,
      "text": "from severe drought conditions in some Mexican states; (ii) if inflation imported from advanced\neconomies is more persistent than expected; (iii) longer-lasting price pressures from supply chain\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 1973,
      "end_char": 2160,
      "text": "6 In its April–June 2019 Quarterly Report, Banxico used a range of models to establish a range between 1.8 and 3.4\npercent for the neutral real policy rate.\nINTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND 13"
    }
  ]
}
